  "query_preview": "(Today through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 1250 AM EST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nToday into Friday\n\nUpper-level analysis shows a pair of highly amplified and negatively \ntilted troughs over the south-central CONUS. The two are nearly \nphased, but not completely so, and may remain un-phased through the \ncourse of the next 24 hours. The primary trough axis extends from \nMissouri southeast to the Florida panhandle.\n\nUpper-level water vapor imagery shows these troughs well, with both \ndisplaying an impressive baroclinic leaf cloud shield as warm moist \nair lifts north ahead of each trough. Strong cyclogenesis is also \nongoing as of this writing over the southeast coast where the \ncoastal baroclinic zone is strongest and jet dynamics are best.\n\nThe trough situated over Missouri is the primary trough, and \ncontains a deeper cold core compared to the trough to its southeast. \nAs such, guidance rapidly develops the surface low over the \nsoutheast before lifting it northwestward into the deeper trough \nduring the occlusion process. This will send a broad shield of \nstratiform precipitation northwestward into Indiana by Thanksgiving \nmorning.\n\nTemperature profiles support rain initially, as warm air advection \nfrom the southeast prevails. However, as the system occludes later \nThursday night into early Friday, dynamic cooling and influence from \nthe cold core aloft may allow for snow to mix in at times. Cold air \nadvection is not anticipated to be a factor in this event and \ntemperatures at the surface are not expected to drop below freezing. \nSnow ratios are likely to be quite low, and current guidance may be \noverdoing it somewhat...showing higher snowfall totals than would be \nexpected during such an event. Dynamic cooling tends to be more \neffective in heavier precipitation rates, which may become more of a \nlimiting factor as the system occludes. Still, wet snowflakes may \nmix in at times after sunset Thursday. A light coating on \ngrassy/elevated surfaces cannot be ruled out but appears to be a low \nprobability scenario as of right now.\n\nIn addition to precipitation, an increasing MSLP gradient from the \napproaching surface low and relatively steep low-level lapse rates \nshould allow for gusty winds at times Thursday. Wind gusts in the 30 \nto 40 mph range appear most probable, peaking during the afternoon \nhours.\n\nPrecipitation may linger into the early afternoon hours on Friday as \nthe now occluded low lifts northeastward. Rainfall totals on average \nrange from 0.75 to 1.25 inches with pockets of higher amounts up to \n1.50 inches possible.\n\nSaturday and Sunday\n\nProgressive flow aloft quickly returns this weekend with an embedded \nvort max ejecting into the Plains early Saturday. This feature races \neastward, with an associated PV anomaly aloft acting upon an east-\nwest oriented baroclinic zone. This enhances frontogenesis along the \ngradient, often leading to heavier banded precipitation. Guidance \nhints at this over the Plains on Saturday, depicting a single band \non the northern edge of a developing surface low. A larger area of \nprecipitation breaks on Sunday out as moisture from the Gulf lifts \nnorth into the developing system. However, the banded nature of \nprecipitation near and to the north of the low may persist into \nIndiana. Thermal profiles show a warm column with above freezing \ntemps through 5000 ft agl. Therefore, the weekend system looks to be \na rain event even on the north side of the low.\n\nMonday and beyond... \n\nCAA will continue Monday albeit weakening in the wake of the cold \nfront/upper trough. Weakening winds with approaching surface high \npressure will set the stage for the coldest night for the next 7 \ndays on Monday night with lows in the low-mid 20s expected most \nareas. Looking at the larger scale pattern, zonal flow will commence \nMonday with weaker shortwave trough energy expected to move across \nthe region midweek. At this time threat for any precip is low.",
